WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.182 --> 00:00:04.245
"Hurry, hurry, hurry! Step right up, ladies and gentlemen! This way to the greatest show on earth. Step inside

2
00:00:04.317 --> 00:00:07.134
and see amazing feats and savage beasts!" For centuries children and adults alike have been attracted to the circus

3
00:00:07.301 --> 00:00:11.300
by these calls, but the first circuses were quite different from the ones we know today. No one is

4
00:00:11.392 --> 00:00:19.401
certain precisely when circuses originated. The ancient Romans were the first people to erect buildings to hold acts

5
00:00:19.403 --> 00:00:22.788
such as chariot races and gladiator battles. Many believe, however, that the Romans were influenced by the exotic animal

6
00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:29.779
exhibitions of ancient Egypt and the market performances of ancient Greece. Circuses have changed quite a bit since those

7
00:00:29.958 --> 00:00:38.447
times. In fact, one of the only things modern circuses have in common with those early performances is the

8
00:00:38.584 --> 00:00:41.551
circular shape of the area in which the acts take place. "Circus" is actually a Latin word that was

9
00:00:41.686 --> 00:00:50.879
derived from the Greek word kirkos , which refers to a circular or oval area enclosed by rows of

10
00:00:51.139 --> 00:01:01.879
seats for spectators. Even though the circus was named after a particular kind of building, circuses were often held

11
00:01:02.672 --> 00:01:11.444
outdoors at fairs as early as the Middle Ages. Farmers and merchants attended these fairs to sell their goods.

12
00:01:11.739 --> 00:01:15.512
Performers quickly discovered that they could also make money at these events; they formed troupes and traveled from

13
00:01:15.513 --> 00:01:22.725
fair to fair. The troupes were generally well received everywhere they went and often acquired new acts from the

14
00:01:22.726 --> 00:01:30.049
territories they traveled through. Chinese acrobats, for example, were in high demand because they incorporated everyday objects like tables,

15
00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:40.824
chairs, and jars into elaborate acrobatic feats of balance. Many Greek performers, who were known for their rope-dancing performances,

16
00:01:40.825 --> 00:01:46.870
also joined the troupes. Fairs continued until the seventeenth century, when the authorities began to restrict performances. They felt

17
00:01:46.871 --> 00:01:52.375
that fairs were responsible for moral pollution throughout England, and allowed thieves and other undesirables to congregate. A few

18
00:01:52.376 --> 00:02:01.090
fairs were still held on special historic occasions, but it wasn’t until 1768, when Philip Astley opened a riding

19
00:02:01.091 --> 00:02:12.405
school in London, that performers started to find work again. Astley had been interested in horses since he was

20
00:02:12.406 --> 00:02:15.854
a boy. He showed a talent for riding from a very early age and was determined to work

21
00:02:15.855 --> 00:02:20.845
with horses. He joined the army’s Fifteenth Light Dragoon Regiment and received a lot of experience and training with

22
00:02:20.846 --> 00:02:27.518
horses during his service. By the time he was discharged, he was a very skilled rider. Astley then moved

23
00:02:27.519 --> 00:02:31.955
to London to make a career out of riding. He knew that riders made little money in England unless

24
00:02:31.956 --> 00:02:35.776
they could do tricks, so he began to learn new ways to ride horses. One day he was riding

25
00:02:35.777 --> 00:02:42.101
his horse at full gallop in a circle and realized that centrifugal force made it much easier for him

26
00:02:42.102 --> 00:02:50.767
to stand on the horse’s back. It was this discovery that led Astley to revive the traditional circus ring

27
00:02:50.768 --> 00:02:57.085
that had been used by the ancient Romans. Astley experimented with different ring sizes to maximize the rider’s

28
00:02:57.086 --> 00:03:01.825
safety. He determined that the optimum ring size for trick riding was 12.8 meters, which has remained the standard

29
00:03:01.826 --> 00:03:10.010
for circuses to this day. London audiences loved Astley’s trick riders, but he was worried that they would get

30
00:03:10.774 --> 00:03:18.153
tired of seeing the same tricks over and over again. So Astley hired jugglers, clowns, musicians, tumblers, and acrobats

31
00:03:18.637 --> 00:03:26.080
to maintain his audience’s interest in the show. Astley became known as the "father of the modern circus." His

32
00:03:26.227 --> 00:03:29.686
troupe became so popular that it performed before France’s King Louis XV. After performing for King Louis, Astley’s circus

33
00:03:29.687 --> 00:03:34.102
traveled extensively throughout Europe. Circuses opened in many cities that Astley and his performers passed through; by the end

34
00:03:34.372 --> 00:03:39.434
of the eighteenth century, circuses existed all over Europe and China. It took a little longer for circuses to

35
00:03:39.506 --> 00:03:48.068
catch on in the United States. It wasn’t until the elephant Old Bet arrived in America that circus

36
00:03:48.857 --> 00:03:52.540
acts became truly popular. Old Bet’s popularity inspired others to exhibit exotic animals. Soon it became common for animal

37
00:03:52.828 --> 00:03:58.544
trainers to risk their lives by performing with tigers, lions, bears, and other wild creatures—even rhinoceroses and hippopotamuses. An

38
00:03:58.545 --> 00:04:03.155
American circus owner, J. Purdy Brown, started housing his shows under a canvas tent, which later became known as

39
00:04:03.266 --> 00:04:14.244
the big top. Many people think that Brown used tents instead of a circular building because they made it

40
00:04:14.245 --> 00:04:23.235
easier for the circus to travel. Circuses gained more attention because they were able to travel to more towns,

41
00:04:23.236 --> 00:04:29.527
and as their popularity grew, so did their size. Then in 1871 Phineas T. Barnum and his partners produced

42
00:04:29.528 --> 00:04:37.464
a circus called the Greatest Show on Earth and transported it by rail, which allowed it to travel

43
00:04:37.465 --> 00:04:49.667
much greater distances. The circus would never be the same. Barnum was no stranger to controversy. He was arrested

44
00:04:49.668 --> 00:04:52.006
three different times for publishing false stories in a Connecticut newspaper. Barnum decided to change professions, but he apparently

45
00:04:52.491 --> 00:04:54.956
enjoyed the notoriety that accompanies controversy. At the age of 24, Barnum presented audiences with what he claimed was

46
00:04:55.229 --> 00:05:00.655
the 161-year-old nurse of George Washington; in actuality the woman was only in her eighties. He then bought the

47
00:05:00.856 --> 00:05:06.639
American Museum, a collection of stuffed animals, wax figures, and other curiosities, in New York City and made a

48
00:05:06.816 --> 00:05:18.301
living off its human and animal oddities—some real, some fake. His best-known fake was the Feejee Mermaid, a creature

49
00:05:18.366 --> 00:05:30.344
with the head of a monkey sewn onto the body of a fish. Barnum incorporated attractions like this into

50
00:05:30.674 --> 00:05:37.783
the Greatest Show on Earth, which led to the tradition of the sideshow in American circuses. Then in

51
00:05:37.991 --> 00:05:45.448
1881 Barnum and the Greatest Show on Earth joined forces with James A. Bailey’s "Great International Circus." The Barnum

52
00:05:46.444 --> 00:05:53.519
& Bailey Circus combined acts that had been established earlier in the nineteenth century with many new attractions. In

53
00:05:53.771 --> 00:06:00.778
fact, they had so many acts in their circus that they had to increase the size of their tents

54
00:06:00.858 --> 00:06:07.075
to accommodate three rings. Together they made their elephant Jumbo into a household name. They also allowed millions of

55
00:06:07.256 --> 00:06:11.477
people over the years to see whole families of trapeze artists flying through the air and other performers eating

56
00:06:11.548 --> 00:06:20.535
fire or swallowing swords. Over time the acts most common in circuses changed. Many circuses got rid of animal

57
00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:24.214
exhibitions in the latter part of the twentieth century due to concerns that the animals were being treated cruelly.

58
00:06:24.431 --> 00:06:32.144
Some decided to focus on human performances that were developed from circus acts pioneered by people like Astley

59
00:06:32.233 --> 00:06:38.920
and Barnum. Contemporary performers such as the Jim Rose Circus, a traveling sideshow, and the acrobatic troupe Cirque du

60
00:06:39.042 --> 00:06:53.370
Soleil agree with Barnum, who said, "Men, women, and children . . . need something to satisfy their gayer,

61
00:06:53.723 --> 00:06:59.636
lighter moods and hours." These modern circuses are certainly providing that "something" to millions of spectators around the world.

